=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, cursor-only, model-effort, resume, same-model-review
intent: Topology in T3 terms. A worker that writes gets its own worktree and a reader shares the checkout; every child runs on this machine, so the list of reasons to stay off a cloud VM becomes a note that runtime checks, transcripts, simulators and local auth work from any worker. Effort stays under the role line's ceiling. Forking a worker becomes sending the new work to that worker with `t3_thread_send`, since T3 has no fork; no rule covers this, so a reviewer may turn it into one. Policy: the verifier runs on its owner's model and effort unless the user names another (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, the three-level cap, the rolling window of about ten, never messaging a worker to check on it, and starting with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Give a worker that writes `isolation: "worktree"`, a checkout of your current local commit, so commit first or name a pushed branch. A worker that only reads gets no `isolation` and shares the checkout, with scratch copies in `/tmp`. Every worker runs on this machine, so a runtime check (the project's own verification skill, the `preview_*` tools, or the shell for a CLI), transcripts, a simulator, and auth that exists only here all work from any worker. Set each subagent's effort for the job per the Subagents section, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Send new work to the worker whose built state it needs,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process, with `t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId`. Otherwise message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the PR's owner, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.

=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, background, store, pr-wait
intent: Policy: reload the orchestrator after auto-compaction. The coordinator keeps `.claude/orchestrator-state.md` in the project current, and the compact hook in references/compact-reload.md (installed in the project's settings.json, since a compact hook in skill frontmatter does not fire) prints it back for the coordinator session only; the frontmatter PreToolUse hook records that session. Upstream's Orchestrate playbook now keeps state in Cursor's Project tracker (`notes.md`) and Project store, which become `notes.md` in the pstack store (rule store), and the state file is rewritten each time a worker reports, since the drain loop is gone. The same paragraph also translates `Task` to async `delegate_task` and the PR and CI event subscriptions to `watch_pull_request`. Still applies while poteto-mode's frontmatter carries the PreToolUse hook.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task` (`mode: "async"`), and keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Keep the program's state in `notes.md` in the store, `~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`. Create it if missing. Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

Keep `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` current too, under 6,000 characters, and rewrite it each time a worker reports. It holds the goal, the path to `notes.md`, each live agent with its `taskId` and `childThreadId`, the open PRs, the decisions waiting on the user, and the next steps. It is the one file the compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints back after compaction. Install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read.
